## The Microsoft Intune App SDK is not part of this package

The SDK is Microsoft's, distributed under Microsoft's own licence terms, and is **not
included in this repository or in the published npm tarball**.

`vendor/` is gitignored and excluded from `package.json`'s `files`. `scripts/fetch-sdks.mjs`
downloads the pinned version at install time, from Microsoft's own releases, verifying a
recorded sha256 per artifact. Nothing about that arrangement grants anyone rights they did
not already have from Microsoft.

Using this package therefore means accepting Microsoft's terms for the SDK. They arrive
alongside the download:

| File | What it covers |
|---|---|
| `vendor/ios/Microsoft License Terms Intune App SDK for iOS.pdf` | the iOS SDK |
| `vendor/android/Microsoft License Terms Intune App SDK for Android.pdf` | the Android SDK |
| `vendor/ios/REDIST.txt` | which iOS binaries may be redistributed in *your* app |
| `vendor/ios/ThirdPartyNotices.txt` | the SDK's own third-party attributions |

Read `REDIST.txt` before shipping: it names the frameworks you are permitted to
redistribute inside an application, which is a narrower permission than redistributing the
SDK itself. This project relies on that permission and does not extend it.

## Microsoft Authentication Library (MSAL)

MSAL is a declared dependency rather than a vendored one — the pod on iOS, the Gradle
artifact on Android — and is fetched from public registries in the normal way. It is
Microsoft's, under the MIT licence, Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation.

The version is pinned in `sdk-versions.json`. The reason is in SPEC §3.1: the Intune SDK
does not carry its own MSAL, it drives the application's, so two MSAL versions in one
binary is a failure nobody debugs quickly.

